Abstract = {Approximately 14\% of the U.S. population relies on private wells for
   drinking water. Little is known about well stewardship, well water
   contaminants and their health impacts, ideal water testing frequency,
   and preferred water treatment. Our mixed-methods study focused on
   identifying well stewardship practices and beliefs in Colorado. We
   contacted septic permit holders and enrolled them in this study if they
   met eligibility criteria. Although annual testing is recommended, only
   8.2\% of study participants had tested their water in the past year, and
   45.5\% of participants reported treating their water. Differences in
   practices and beliefs were observed based on income and education
   levels. Identified gaps in knowledge, testing, and treatment suggest
   that public-private partnerships and increased development of resources,
   annual reminders, and better access to testing might improve stewardship
   and the overall health of well users. Well users could benefit from
   increased access to resources about well stewardship-specifically
   testing and treating well water-to improve drinking water safety.},
